#* {{quote-journal|en|title=''The Glory of Christ in the Creation and Reconciliation of All Things.'' With Special Reference to the Doctrine of Eternal Evil. A Course of Sermons Preached at Eaton Chapel, Eaton Square, London. By Samuel Minton,{{nb...|M.A., of Worcester College, Oxford}}. Second Edition. London: Longman, Green & Co. [book review]|editor=William Leask|magazine=The Rainbow: A Magazine of Christian Literature, with Special Reference to the Revealed Future of the Church and the World|location=London|publisher=W. Kent & Co.,{{nb...|Paternoster Row, E.C.}}|date=1 September 1869|volume=VI|issue=69|page=432|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=CzEEAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA432|column=1|oclc=39520061|passage=That neither Dr. Leask, nor myself, acted on such a principle, is the whole '''gravamen''' of our opponent's furious assaults upon us.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Sidney H. Willig|chapter=Impact of Drug Products Liability on Needs for Pharmacoepidemiologic Studies and Expertise|editor=Stanley A. Edlavitch|title=Pharmacoepidemiology|location=Chelsea, Mich.|publisher=[[w:CRC Press|Lewis Publishers]]|year=1989|volume=I|section=section 2 (Drug Epidemiology and the Law)|pages=62–63|pageurl=https://books.google.comg/books?id=ND5b6D0NcLcC&pg=PA63|isbn=978-0-87371-129-6|passage=Prescription drug suppliers are more likely to add even unverified reports that stand as aberrations in pharmacoepidemiologic studies when they associate a use of the product with an appreciable '''gravamen''' of injury.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Lawrence J. Smith|Lawrence J[ack] Smith]]|title=Handgun Violence Prevention Act of 1989: Hearing before the Subcommittee on Crime of the [[w:United States House Committee on the Judiciary|Committee on the Judiciary]], [[w:United States House of Representatives|House of Representatives]], One Hundred First [[w:United States Congress|Congress]], Second Session on H.R. 993: Handgun Violence Prevention Act of 1989{{nb...|Printed for the Use of the Committee on the Judiciary}}|series=Serial No. 93|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=[[w:United States Government Publishing Office|U.S. Government Printing Office]]|date=26 April 1990|page=25|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=CvInAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA25|oclc=869428235|passage=The grit of your argument—'''gravamen'''; I haven't used that in a long time since I practiced—the '''gravamen''' of your argument is that availability is the test, the key. {{...}} Tell me where is the '''gravamen''' of your argument?}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=[[w:Eric S. Raymond|Eric S[teven] Raymond]]|title=SF Words and Prototype Worlds|work=SF Words|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20210507150919/http://www.catb.org/~esr/sf-words/essay.html|archivedate=7 May 2021|date=31 July 2002|passage=The '''gravamen''' of this essay is that these signifiers (the jargon of SF [science fiction]) function not merely as a set of isolated signs but as descriptions of a stock set of ''prototype worlds'' which they logically and conventionally imply, and which permit writers to specify mainly what (if anything) is ''unique'' about their world vision rather than what is shared with the rest of SF.}}
